polarizedK3surface -- polarized K3 surface associated to a doubly special cubic fourfold
****************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        E = polarizedK3surface X
  * Inputs:
      * X, a *note doubly special cubic fourfold: DoublySpecialCubicFourfold,,
        whose defining union of surfaces admits a *note congruence:
        CongruenceOfCurves, of curves
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * FanoMapType => ..., default value null
      * Strategy => ..., default value null
      * *note Verbose: Verbose, => ..., default value false, request verbose
        feedback
  * Outputs:
      * E, the polarized K3 surface associated to $X$

Description
===========

At the first call, this function initializes a container object: it computes
the underlying *note projective surface:
(MultiprojectiveVarieties)EmbeddedProjectiveVariety, but leaves the lattice
polarization data empty to save time.

The full lattice data is computed and stored within the object only upon a
second call. Once initialized, *note polarize: (Macaulay2Doc)polarize, can be
used as a synonym (using default options).

The following methods can be used to access the construction data of $E$:

  * building E -- returns the four construction objects as in *note
    associatedK3surface(CubicFourfold): associatedK3surface_lpCubicFourfold_rp,
    (the Fano rational map $\mu$, the non-minimal K3 surface $U$, the
    exceptional curves on $U$ and the morphism $f: U \to E$ contracting them);
  * projectiveVariety E -- returns the underlying *note projective surface:
    (MultiprojectiveVarieties)EmbeddedProjectiveVariety,;
  * latticePolarization E -- returns the lattice data induced by the doubly
    special structure of $X$.

Optional inputs:

  * *note Strategy: (Macaulay2Doc)Strategy, -- provides instructions for each
    step. The first stage handles the construction of the non-minimal K3
    surface $U$ by inverting the Fano map restricted to the cubic (via
    "Inverse" or "Approximate"), while the second computes the lattice data
    (common options include "MapFromW", "MapFromU", or "SpecialCurve"). Both
    can be passed at once as a sequence, e.g., Strategy => ("Inverse",
    "MapFromW"), or provided individually through nested calls.
  * *note FanoMapType: polarizedK3surface, -- sets the Fano map to "Standard"
    (default) or "P2xP2". Note that switching this type updates the global
    behavior of $X$ (e.g., affecting methods like *note mirrorFourfold:
    mirrorFourfold,), though previously computed data is preserved;
  * *note Verbose: (Macaulay2Doc)Verbose, -- if set to *note true:
    (Macaulay2Doc)true,, provides feedback during the construction.
